    "title": "The Same Five Windows, Any Minor Key",
    "summary": "The whole ladder is anchored on the root's fret on the low E string, so a key change moves every number and changes nothing about the structure — and the chords of the key move with it.",
    "tags": ["caged", "minor"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "For five chapters this pathway has worked in one key: A minor, with C major beside it. Every fret number in it has been one of those two. That was a teaching choice, not a limit — and here is where it lifts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The anchor makes everything else cheap. The "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " barre at fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the E form, and fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is where "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits on the low E string. Find any key's root on that same string and the E form's window runs from one fret below it to three frets above it; the other four forms follow from there. Check it against two keys you can verify without a table: "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits at fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and E minor's E form is "
