    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every cycle in that table is the same loop — "
        },
        {
          "text": "C→A→G→E→D→C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — entered at a different point. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Same Ladder in Any Key",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "caged-transposing-the-ladder"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already proved that for the major forms, and it's identical here for the reason chapter 2 opened on: a window is anchored on the root and never knew about the chord's quality."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "What changes in minor is worth a paragraph of its own. The form named after the key sits at the nut in five keys — "
        },
        {
          "text": "A, C, D, E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " minor — but only three of those are chords you can play open: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "Em",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "Dm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". There's no open "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " or "
        },
        {
          "text": "Cm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and the G form of a minor chord can't be barred at all. So in G minor, the form sitting at the nut is the one form you can never hold whole — a sentence the major version of this story never has to say."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The rest of the keys are worth one honest sentence: a key with no letter form of its own enters the wheel between two letters, and its nut-most window is whichever one lands there. B minor's is the C form, frets "
        },
        {
          "text": "0–3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". That's the one example worth naming, and it stops there — there's no general rule for which form sits nearest the nut, and none is claimed here."
        }
      ]
    },
