    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The relative major moves with the key too, as the table's last column shows — always a minor third above the minor root, chapter 1's rule, still true."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A word on the flats: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "Ab",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " are written flat because those are flat keys. This pathway has spelled everything with sharps until now; this is the one place that changes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One more caution, and it matters for any diagram you read elsewhere: near the nut a window can be cut short. A minor's A form is only four frets, "
        },
        {
          "text": "0–3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", not five — the nut cuts it off, same as it always has. In some keys the nut-most window is cut shorter still: F# minor's is just three frets, "
        },
        {
          "text": "0–2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Same form, less room to draw it in — never call one of those a five-fret window."
        }
      ]
    },
